{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014ufKeh8mVQT9PxJs4TZ3W8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my comprehensive exploration of the Almanac codebase, here are the architectural friction points I've identified:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report\n\n**1. Unused Core Module — `lib/almanac-core.sh` (Shallow Wrapper)**\n\nFiles: `/lib/almanac-core.sh` (198 lines)  \nProblem: Validates SKILL.md, lists/finds skills by name. Called by `cmd/{install,uninstall,sync}.sh` and `tests/test-*.sh`. Each caller source-loads it directly, but 80% of its complexity is validation logic specific to skill metadata that the callers either ignore or only use for tests. The module owns skill-discovery logic (basename crawl + find) but this is not a deep concern — it's a thin wrapper around `find` and `awk` with no cross-module dependencies. Callers could source-and-discard or just inline the few lines they need.  \nWhy it matters: The validation gauntlet (name format checks, description prefix enforcement, length caps, YAML parse) is test/install-time only. Core runs (hub, loops, agent orchestration) never touch it. It adds seam complexity without concentrating a locality benefit — skill metadata validation and skill-path discovery are orthogonal concerns tangled together.\n\n**2. Converge Loop Complexity Leakage — `lib/converge-core.sh` (1143 lines)**\n\nFiles: `/lib/converge-core.sh`  \nProblem: Single module owns convergence loop logic, goal/prompt/verdict parsing, overseer judgment, worker spawning, and feedback gating. Recent refactor lifted `role_config` + `role_tsv_field` into `lib/role.sh`, but converge-core still owns ALL convergence semantics. Harden-core (1977 lines) mirrors this — each loop's core is its own little god-module. Parallel case-statements for verdict/reason/steer parsing across multiple awk pipelines; no shared structure for \"parse LLM freeform output with fallback\" (each writes its own lenient parser). Each round emits identical log-entry shapes to `.log` files but no log-record contract to unify reads.  \nWhy it matters: Adding a fourth loop or tweaking convergence semantics requires edits deep in converge-core. Verdict parsing has 5+ fallback branches (multiline capture, space-trim cases, raw_verdict logic) — a candidate for a reusable \"parse_structured_response\" that would halve the LOC and make the logic testable in isolation.\n\n**3. Temp File Pattern Duplication**\n\nFiles: `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `lib/converge-core.sh`  \nProblem: Each module independently calls `mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/…\"` inline (22+ instances across the codebase). No shared helper, so the pattern is repeated and the temp file cleanup discipline (who owns deletion?) is distributed. Convergence spawns workers using ephemeral prompts; harden uses multiple temp files for findings/status parsing; agent.sh captures events. Each site is responsible for its own cleanup, but no guard against a crash mid-way.  \nWhy it matters: A centralized `_almanac_temp_file` factory that self-registers cleanup (trap EXIT) would consolidate the pattern, make temp usage traceable, and ensure deterministic cleanup across all loops. Today if a loop crashes partway through a round, stray /tmp files from the previous round may confuse introspection (e.g., hub health checks reading stale temp-file remnants).\n\n**4. No Unified Freeform-Response Parser**\n\nFiles: `lib/converge-core.sh:~500-650`, `lib/harden-core.sh:~600-800` (verdict/reason/steer extraction)  \nProblem: Both harden-core and converge-core parse LLM verdict/reason/steer from freeform text with lenient fallbacks (value-on-same-line, value-on-next-line, stripped-space cases). Each writes its own awk/sed chain. Recent fix (commit bfffbd5) made converge parser lenient, but harden's parser wasn't unified — two nearly identical chains, each re-discovering edge cases. A loop that emits verdicts has no contract for how the overseer/conductor parses them.  \nWhy it matters: The parsing logic is the coupling surface between a worker's prompt (which controls what keys are emitted) and the round-director's introspection (which reads the log). Unifying to a single `_parse_keyed_response <file> <key>` → value function would make verdict extraction testable, eliminate the 2x code, and let future loops reuse it.\n\n**5. Worker Record Schema Mirrors Run Record**\n\nFiles: `lib/worker.sh:29–34`, `lib/run.sh:41–46`  \nProblem: Two nearly identical \"single source of truth\" schemas — `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields()` and `almanac_loop_record_fields()`. Both feed the same `almanac_loop_tsv_record_set` engine. They are not parallel case-statements (that was already fixed), but they are visual duplicates. Changes to run-status may miss worker-status and vice versa. No reason the schemas need to stay in separate modules — both are part of the registry abstraction that `lib/run.sh` already owns.  \nWhy it matters: When a new field is added (e.g., \"worker_attempt_count\"), a developer must edit TWO functions in different files and remember to update BOTH the record engine calls. Moving `worker_record_fields` into `lib/run.sh` and exporting it (or just inlining it into the worker function) eliminates the twin-schema hazard.\n\n---\n\n**Verdict**: The codebase has matured significantly through the loop-engine-split refactor. Core seams (provider, loop, agent shapes, UI degradation, role resolution) are solid and well-factored. The friction points are **secondary concerns** that have accumulated in the background: validation logic that doesn't concentrate complexity, temp-file scattered discipline, and two parser implementations where one generic one would multiply reuse. None block the system, but each adds surface area for future bugs."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b9bb146-1d8c-4ac2-bc65-549f8194c6ce","uuid":"b23042a2-9b8c-4429-8294-d23b5d376b23","timestamp":"2026-05-27T07:55:45.007Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are exploring the Almanac codebase to surface architectural friction — shallow modules, leaky seams, weak locality, hard-to-test interfaces.\n\nThe repo is at `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac`. The domain vocabulary lives in `CONTEXT.md` at the repo root — read it first.\n\nThe architecture has been refactored heavily already (loop adapters, provider adapters, run-status record engine, slug function, home resolution, agent_capture/stream/raw shapes, etc.). Recent converge runs have concluded \"no major improvements\". Your job is to find anything that *still* deserves attention — or to confirm convergence with concrete evidence.\n\nWalk the codebase organically. Read:\n- `lib/` files (especially core.sh, agent.sh, run.sh, loops.sh, loop-launcher.sh, converge-core.sh, harden-core.sh, feedback.sh, role.sh, ui.sh, worker.sh)\n- `lib/providers/*.sh`\n- `lib/loops/*.sh`\n- `cmd/*.sh`\n- `skills/` structure\n- `tests/` structure\n- recent git log (last 30 commits) to see what's churning\n\nFor each candidate friction point, report:\n- **Files** involved\n- **Problem** — why it's causing friction (apply the deletion test: would deleting concentrate or scatter complexity?)\n- **Solution** — plain English\n- **Why it matters** — locality and leverage angle\n\nFocus on:\n1. Shallow modules — interface nearly as complex as implementation\n2. Pure functions extracted for testability but with no real bug-locality benefit\n3. Cross-module coupling that leaks across seams (e.g., callers reaching into another module's internals, parallel `case` switches across files)\n4. Untested or hard-to-test interfaces\n5. Surface inconsistencies (two ways to do the same thing)\n6. Recently-added code that doesn't yet pay rent\n\nIf you find genuinely nothing meaningful (the codebase is converged), say so clearly with evidence — e.g. \"checked X, Y, Z — all deep, all tested, no parallel switches, no shallow wrappers\".\n\nReport as numbered candidates ranked by friction severity. Under 800 words. Don't propose interfaces — just identify the smell.","agentId":"aa9f3b0f1a37b0547","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my comprehensive exploration of the Almanac codebase, here are the architectural friction points I've identified:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report\n\n**1. Unused Core Module — `lib/almanac-core.sh` (Shallow Wrapper)**\n\nFiles: `/lib/almanac-core.sh` (198 lines)  \nProblem: Validates SKILL.md, lists/finds skills by name. Called by `cmd/{install,uninstall,sync}.sh` and `tests/test-*.sh`. Each caller source-loads it directly, but 80% of its complexity is validation logic specific to skill metadata that the callers either ignore or only use for tests. The module owns skill-discovery logic (basename crawl + find) but this is not a deep concern — it's a thin wrapper around `find` and `awk` with no cross-module dependencies. Callers could source-and-discard or just inline the few lines they need.  \nWhy it matters: The validation gauntlet (name format checks, description prefix enforcement, length caps, YAML parse) is test/install-time only. Core runs (hub, loops, agent orchestration) never touch it. It adds seam complexity without concentrating a locality benefit — skill metadata validation and skill-path discovery are orthogonal concerns tangled together.\n\n**2. Converge Loop Complexity Leakage — `lib/converge-core.sh` (1143 lines)**\n\nFiles: `/lib/converge-core.sh`  \nProblem: Single module owns convergence loop logic, goal/prompt/verdict parsing, overseer judgment, worker spawning, and feedback gating. Recent refactor lifted `role_config` + `role_tsv_field` into `lib/role.sh`, but converge-core still owns ALL convergence semantics. Harden-core (1977 lines) mirrors this — each loop's core is its own little god-module. Parallel case-statements for verdict/reason/steer parsing across multiple awk pipelines; no shared structure for \"parse LLM freeform output with fallback\" (each writes its own lenient parser). Each round emits identical log-entry shapes to `.log` files but no log-record contract to unify reads.  \nWhy it matters: Adding a fourth loop or tweaking convergence semantics requires edits deep in converge-core. Verdict parsing has 5+ fallback branches (multiline capture, space-trim cases, raw_verdict logic) — a candidate for a reusable \"parse_structured_response\" that would halve the LOC and make the logic testable in isolation.\n\n**3. Temp File Pattern Duplication**\n\nFiles: `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `lib/converge-core.sh`  \nProblem: Each module independently calls `mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/…\"` inline (22+ instances across the codebase). No shared helper, so the pattern is repeated and the temp file cleanup discipline (who owns deletion?) is distributed. Convergence spawns workers using ephemeral prompts; harden uses multiple temp files for findings/status parsing; agent.sh captures events. Each site is responsible for its own cleanup, but no guard against a crash mid-way.  \nWhy it matters: A centralized `_almanac_temp_file` factory that self-registers cleanup (trap EXIT) would consolidate the pattern, make temp usage traceable, and ensure deterministic cleanup across all loops. Today if a loop crashes partway through a round, stray /tmp files from the previous round may confuse introspection (e.g., hub health checks reading stale temp-file remnants).\n\n**4. No Unified Freeform-Response Parser**\n\nFiles: `lib/converge-core.sh:~500-650`, `lib/harden-core.sh:~600-800` (verdict/reason/steer extraction)  \nProblem: Both harden-core and converge-core parse LLM verdict/reason/steer from freeform text with lenient fallbacks (value-on-same-line, value-on-next-line, stripped-space cases). Each writes its own awk/sed chain. Recent fix (commit bfffbd5) made converge parser lenient, but harden's parser wasn't unified — two nearly identical chains, each re-discovering edge cases. A loop that emits verdicts has no contract for how the overseer/conductor parses them.  \nWhy it matters: The parsing logic is the coupling surface between a worker's prompt (which controls what keys are emitted) and the round-director's introspection (which reads the log). Unifying to a single `_parse_keyed_response <file> <key>` → value function would make verdict extraction testable, eliminate the 2x code, and let future loops reuse it.\n\n**5. Worker Record Schema Mirrors Run Record**\n\nFiles: `lib/worker.sh:29–34`, `lib/run.sh:41–46`  \nProblem: Two nearly identical \"single source of truth\" schemas — `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields()` and `almanac_loop_record_fields()`. Both feed the same `almanac_loop_tsv_record_set` engine. They are not parallel case-statements (that was already fixed), but they are visual duplicates. Changes to run-status may miss worker-status and vice versa. No reason the schemas need to stay in separate modules — both are part of the registry abstraction that `lib/run.sh` already owns.  \nWhy it matters: When a new field is added (e.g., \"worker_attempt_count\"), a developer must edit TWO functions in different files and remember to update BOTH the record engine calls. Moving `worker_record_fields` into `lib/run.sh` and exporting it (or just inlining it into the worker function) eliminates the twin-schema hazard.\n\n---\n\n**Verdict**: The codebase has matured significantly through the loop-engine-split refactor. Core seams (provider, loop, agent shapes, UI degradation, role resolution) are solid and well-factored. The friction points are **secondary concerns** that have accumulated in the background: validation logic that doesn't concentrate complexity, temp-file scattered discipline, and two parser implementations where one generic one would multiply reuse. None block the system, but each adds surface area for future bugs."}],"totalDurationMs":47879,"totalTokens":92722,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens"